Chapter: E1000e
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 Intel gigabit Ethernet, the 8254x/8257x/I21x descriptor-ring family.

 This is the driver for a part we do not own, so what is written down
 here is the part number, the register offset, the bit position and what
 the silicon does with each -- none of which a reader can derive from the
 code beside it. Everything this driver decides for itself is left to the
 code.

Section: Identification

 Every Intel network part answers vendor 0x8086, and the ones this
 driver speaks to answer class 0x02 subclass 0x00 (Ethernet). The
 device ID is NOT pinned here on purpose. The register layout below is
 common to the family, and the exact part on any given board is a
 question for the PCI probe rather than an assumption in the source;
 an I219-V answers 0x15B8, an I219-LM 0x15B7, an 82574L 0x10D3, and a
 driver that named one of them would refuse the other two for no
 reason the hardware requires.

  e1000-vendor-intel : Integer = #8086
  e1000-class-network : Integer = 2
  e1000-subclass-ethernet : Integer = 0

  e1000-find : PciScanResult -> Maybe PciDevice
  e1000-find (scan) = e1000-find-loop (scan.devices) 0 (scan.count)

  e1000-find-loop : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer -> Maybe PciDevice
  e1000-find-loop (devs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then None
   else let d = list-at devs i
   in if e1000-is-candidate d then Just d
   else e1000-find-loop devs (i + 1) len

  e1000-is-candidate : PciDevice -> Boolean
  e1000-is-candidate (d) =
   if (d.pci-vendor) /= e1000-vendor-intel then False
   else if (d.pci-class) /= e1000-class-network then False
   else (d.pci-subclass) == e1000-subclass-ethernet

Section: Register Offsets

 Byte offsets into the BAR 0 memory window. These are the device's, not
 ours, and they are stable across the whole family.

  e1000-reg-ctrl : Integer = #0000
  e1000-reg-status : Integer = #0008
  e1000-reg-ctrl-ext : Integer = #0018
  e1000-reg-icr : Integer = #00C0
  e1000-reg-imc : Integer = #00D8
  e1000-reg-rctl : Integer = #0100
  e1000-reg-tctl : Integer = #0400
  e1000-reg-tipg : Integer = #0410
  e1000-reg-rdbal : Integer = #2800
  e1000-reg-rdbah : Integer = #2804
  e1000-reg-rdlen : Integer = #2808
  e1000-reg-rdh : Integer = #2810
  e1000-reg-rdt : Integer = #2818
  e1000-reg-tdbal : Integer = #3800
  e1000-reg-tdbah : Integer = #3804
  e1000-reg-tdlen : Integer = #3808
  e1000-reg-tdh : Integer = #3810
  e1000-reg-tdt : Integer = #3818
  e1000-reg-mta : Integer = #5200
  e1000-reg-ral : Integer = #5400
  e1000-reg-rah : Integer = #5404
  e1000-reg-mdic : Integer = #0020

 CTRL bits. RST is self-clearing and takes microseconds; SLU asks the
 MAC to bring the link up and ASDE lets it settle speed and duplex from
 the PHY's own auto-negotiation, which is why this driver never writes a
 speed.

  e1000-ctrl-slu : Integer = #00000040
  e1000-ctrl-asde : Integer = #00000020
  e1000-ctrl-rst : Integer = #04000000

 STATUS bit 1 is LU, link up. It reads zero until auto-negotiation
 finishes, which is the only reason link bring-up needs a wait at all.

  e1000-status-lu : Integer = #00000002

 RCTL. BAM accepts broadcast, which ARP needs. UPE and MPE are
 promiscuous unicast and multicast: they are set during bring-up
 deliberately, because the first honest proof that this driver receives
 is a frame that was already on the wire, and a MAC filter that is
 subtly wrong looks exactly like a driver that does not receive. SECRC
 strips the four-byte CRC so the length the descriptor reports is the
 length of the frame. BSIZE is left at its zero setting, which is 2048
 bytes and matches e1000-buf-size below.

  e1000-rctl-en : Integer = #00000002
  e1000-rctl-upe : Integer = #00000008
  e1000-rctl-mpe : Integer = #00000010
  e1000-rctl-bam : Integer = #00008000
  e1000-rctl-secrc : Integer = #04000000

 TCTL. CT is the collision threshold and COLD the collision distance;
 the values below are the 802.3 full-duplex figures, 15 and 64, at bit
 positions 4 and 12.

  e1000-tctl-en : Integer = #00000002
  e1000-tctl-psp : Integer = #00000008
  e1000-tctl-ct : Integer = #000000F0
  e1000-tctl-cold : Integer = #00040000

 TIPG carries three inter-packet-gap fields: IPGT at bit 0, IPGR1 at
 bit 10, IPGR2 at bit 20. 10, 8 and 6 are the copper 802.3 values.

  e1000-tipg-default : Integer = #0060200A

 Descriptor status bits, in the status byte of a receive descriptor.
 DD says the hardware is done with the descriptor and the driver may
 read it; EOP says the frame ends here rather than continuing into the
 next descriptor.

  e1000-rx-status-dd : Integer = #01
  e1000-rx-status-eop : Integer = #02

 Transmit command bits. EOP ends the packet, IFCS asks the hardware to
 append the Ethernet CRC, and RS asks it to report completion by setting
 DD in the descriptor's own status byte -- without RS the driver has
 nothing to wait on.

  e1000-tx-cmd-eop : Integer = #01
  e1000-tx-cmd-ifcs : Integer = #02
  e1000-tx-cmd-rs : Integer = #08
  e1000-tx-status-dd : Integer = #01

Section: Ring Geometry

 A descriptor is sixteen bytes and the hardware requires the ring base
 to be sixteen-byte aligned and its length in bytes to be a multiple of
 128. Sixteen descriptors of sixteen bytes is 256, which satisfies both.

  e1000-desc-size : Integer = 16
  e1000-rx-count : Integer = 16
  e1000-tx-count : Integer = 16
  e1000-buf-size : Integer = 2048
  e1000-max-frame : Integer = 1522

Section: Where the BAR Is Allowed to Land

 The runtime page tables decide this and the driver only reports it.
 Three gigabytes are mapped identity as RAM and are the heap and stack
 arena; the single page directory above that maps three to four
 gigabytes for devices; nothing above four gigabytes is mapped at all.

 So a memory BAR has three possible fates and only one of them works.
 Inside the device window it is reachable. Above four gigabytes there is
 no mapping and the first register read takes a page fault the demand
 handler will not serve. Below three gigabytes is the case that looks
 harmless and is not: the address IS mapped, so nothing faults, but it
 is mapped as ordinary RAM inside the arena the allocator hands out, so
 the driver and the heap are pointed at the same bytes.

 That third case is not hypothetical. Firmware places a BAR wherever it
 finds a hole, and a machine with less RAM has its hole lower down: OVMF
 was measured putting a NIC BAR at 0x81060000, which is 2.16 GB and
 squarely inside the arena.

 Refusing is the whole handling. A driver that proceeds from a bad
 window does not fail at the point of the mistake -- it either writes
 registers into its own heap and reads back whatever it last allocated,
 or it faults several calls later inside a ring walk, and neither
 failure names the BAR.

  e1000-window-lo : Integer = 3221225472
  e1000-window-hi : Integer = 4294967296

  e1000-bar-ok : Integer = 0
  e1000-bar-is-io : Integer = 1
  e1000-bar-below-window : Integer = 2
  e1000-bar-above-window : Integer = 3
  e1000-bar-zero : Integer = 4

  e1000-bar-verdict : PciBar -> Integer
  e1000-bar-verdict (bar) =
   if (bar.bar-is-io) then e1000-bar-is-io
   else if (bar.bar-base) == 0 then e1000-bar-zero
   else if (bar.bar-base) >= e1000-window-hi then e1000-bar-above-window
   else if (bar.bar-base) < e1000-window-lo then e1000-bar-below-window
   else e1000-bar-ok

Section: Device Handle

 Allocated once at bring-up and never rebuilt. The cursors that move
 per packet live in the control block rather than in this record,
 because a record rebuilt on every poll would allocate on every poll,
 and the receive path is polled millions of times per accept with no
 collector behind it.

  E1000Device = record {
    e-mmio : Integer,
    e-rx-ring : Integer,
    e-tx-ring : Integer,
    e-rx-bufs : Integer,
    e-tx-bufs : Integer,
    e-ctrl-blk : Integer,
    e-present : Boolean,
    e-mac : List Integer,
    e-mac-valid : Boolean
  }

 The control block holds two cursors: the next receive descriptor this
 driver will examine, and the next transmit descriptor it will fill.

  e1000-cb-rx-next : Integer = 0
  e1000-cb-tx-next : Integer = 4
  e1000-cb-size : Integer = 16

Section: Register Access

 Identity paging makes a virtual address its own physical address, so a
 BAR value is usable as written and a buffer address is usable as a DMA
 target without translation.

  e1000-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-read (mmio) (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (mmio + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  e1000-write : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-write (mmio) (off) (v) = poke-32 (mmio + off) 0 v

Section: Memory

 alloc-bytes is a bump of the heap pointer and does not zero what it
 returns, so every descriptor field this driver relies on is written
 explicitly. It also gives no alignment guarantee, so a ring over-
 allocates by one alignment unit and rounds the base up.

  e1000-align : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-align (addr) (n) =
   let r = int-mod addr n
   in if r == 0 then addr else addr + (n - r)

  e1000-alloc-aligned : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-alloc-aligned (bytes) (align) =
   let raw = alloc-bytes (bytes + align)
   in e1000-align raw align

  e1000-zero-words : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-zero-words (base) (count) (i) =
   if i >= count then 0
   else let w = poke-32 (base + i * 4) 0 0
   in e1000-zero-words base count (i + 1)

Section: Reset and Link

 The reset sequence the datasheet asks for: mask every interrupt so a
 half-configured device cannot raise one, pulse RST, wait for the bit to
 clear itself, then mask again because the reset restores the interrupt
 mask to its power-on state.

  e1000-reset : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-reset (mmio) =
   let m1 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
   in let ctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let r = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-or ctrl e1000-ctrl-rst)
   in let settled = e1000-await-reset mmio 0
   in let m2 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
   in let cleared = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-icr
   in settled

 Every wait in this chapter is fuel-capped rather than unbounded. A
 device that never clears its reset bit is a device that is absent or
 wedged, and a driver that spins forever on it takes the machine down
 with it.

  e1000-reset-fuel : Integer = 1000000

  e1000-await-reset : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-reset (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-reset-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl) e1000-ctrl-rst == 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-reset mmio (i + 1)

  e1000-link-fuel : Integer = 4000000

 MDIC is the only door to the PHY. On the 8254x the PHY is a discrete part
 on an MDIO bus; on the I219 the MAC and PHY are both inside the PCH and
 this same register reaches it. Software writes MDIC with the address, the
 opcode and (for a write) the data, leaving R clear, then polls until the
 MAC sets R. E is the MAC saying the transaction failed, which is a
 different answer from a read of zero and must not be folded into one:
 nothing listening at an address reads as E, while a register genuinely
 holding zero reads as R with zero data.

  e1000-mdic-op-read : Integer = #08000000
  e1000-mdic-op-write : Integer = #04000000
  e1000-mdic-r : Integer = #10000000
  e1000-mdic-e : Integer = #40000000
  e1000-mdic-data : Integer = #0000FFFF
  e1000-mdic-fuel : Integer = 100000

 The PHY answers at one address on this family.

  e1000-phy-addr : Integer = 1
  e1000-phy-bmcr : Integer = 0
  e1000-phy-bmsr : Integer = 1
  e1000-phy-id1 : Integer = 2

  e1000-bmcr-reset : Integer = #8000
  e1000-bmcr-aneg-en : Integer = #1000
  e1000-bmcr-aneg-rst : Integer = #0200
  e1000-bmsr-aneg-done : Integer = #0020

  e1000-mdic-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-mdic-frame (op) (phy) (reg) (data) =
   bit-or op (bit-or (bit-shl phy 21)
    (bit-or (bit-shl reg 16) (bit-and data e1000-mdic-data)))

 Answers negative one for a PHY that never reports ready and for one that
 reports an error, and the caller must not treat either as a register
 value. A sixteen-bit register cannot hold negative one, so the sentinel
 cannot collide with a legitimate reading.

 The address is a parameter rather than baked into the frame so that a
 caller can ask an address nothing answers on and get the error sentinel
 back. Without that a test cannot tell an addressed transaction from one
 whose address field is ignored, and both look identical when the only
 address ever used is the right one.

  e1000-phy-read-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-read-at (mmio) (phy) (reg) =
   let f = e1000-mdic-frame e1000-mdic-op-read phy reg 0
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-mdic f
   in e1000-await-mdic mmio 0

  e1000-phy-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-read (mmio) (reg) = e1000-phy-read-at mmio e1000-phy-addr reg

  e1000-phy-write : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-write (mmio) (reg) (data) =
   let f = e1000-mdic-frame e1000-mdic-op-write e1000-phy-addr reg data
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-mdic f
   in e1000-await-mdic mmio 0

  e1000-await-mdic : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-mdic (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-mdic-fuel then (0 - 1)
   else let v = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-mdic
    in if bit-and v e1000-mdic-e /= 0 then (0 - 1)
    else if bit-and v e1000-mdic-r /= 0 then bit-and v e1000-mdic-data
    else e1000-await-mdic mmio (i + 1)

 Reset the PHY and restart auto-negotiation before asking the MAC for a
 link. On a part with a discrete PHY the MAC's own SLU is usually enough
 and this is redundant; on a PCH-integrated part it is not, and the
 failure when it is skipped is a link that simply never comes up, with
 every MAC register reading exactly as it should. That is why this runs
 unconditionally rather than behind a device check: the cost is a handful
 of MDIC transactions and the alternative is a silent failure on the one
 board we have.

 A PHY that cannot be reached is NOT fatal here. e1000-phy-read answers
 negative one and this returns zero, and the caller still sets SLU: a part
 whose PHY we cannot talk to may still bring a link up on its own, and
 refusing it outright would be worse than trying. What the driver must not
 do is report success it did not observe.

  e1000-phy-bring-up : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-bring-up (mmio) =
   let r = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-bmcr e1000-bmcr-reset
   in if r < 0 then 0
   else let mode = bit-or e1000-bmcr-aneg-en e1000-bmcr-aneg-rst
    in let a = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-bmcr mode
    in if a < 0 then 0
    else e1000-await-aneg mmio 0

  e1000-aneg-fuel : Integer = 1000000

  e1000-await-aneg : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-aneg (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-aneg-fuel then 0
   else let s = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-bmsr
    in if s < 0 then 0
    else if bit-and s e1000-bmsr-aneg-done /= 0 then 1
    else e1000-await-aneg mmio (i + 1)

  e1000-link-up : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-link-up (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-bring-up mmio
   in let ctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-or ctrl (bit-or e1000-ctrl-slu e1000-ctrl-asde))
   in e1000-await-link mmio 0

  e1000-await-link : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-link (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-link-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status) e1000-status-lu /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-link mmio (i + 1)

  e1000-has-link : E1000Device -> Boolean
  e1000-has-link (d) =
   if (d.e-present) then bit-and (e1000-read (d.e-mmio) e1000-reg-status) e1000-status-lu /= 0
   else False

Section: Station Address

 RAL and RAH are loaded from the part's own NVM before the driver runs,
 and RAH bit 31 is AV, address valid. A part whose AV bit is clear has
 not been given an address, and this driver reports that rather than
 inventing one: a fabricated MAC would let every later stage look
 healthy while the network refused to answer it.

  e1000-rah-av : Integer = #80000000

  e1000-mac-present : Integer -> Boolean
  e1000-mac-present (mmio) = bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rah) e1000-rah-av /= 0

  e1000-read-mac : Integer -> List Integer
  e1000-read-mac (mmio) =
   let lo = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ral
   in let hi = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rah
   in [bit-and lo #FF, bit-and (bit-shru lo 8) #FF,
       bit-and (bit-shru lo 16) #FF, bit-and (bit-shru lo 24) #FF,
       bit-and hi #FF, bit-and (bit-shru hi 8) #FF]

Section: Receive Ring

 Each descriptor is given a buffer address and cleared status. The tail
 is set one behind the head, which is what hands every descriptor in the
 ring to the hardware: the region between head and tail is the driver's,
 and everything else is the device's to fill.

  e1000-setup-rx : E1000Device -> Integer
  e1000-setup-rx (d) =
   let mmio = d.e-mmio
   in let built = e1000-build-rx-descs d 0
   in let bl = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdbal (bit-and (d.e-rx-ring) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let bh = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdbah (bit-shru (d.e-rx-ring) 32)
   in let ln = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdlen (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size)
   in let h = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdh 0
   in let t = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdt (e1000-rx-count - 1)
   in let cur = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0 0
   in let promisc = bit-or e1000-rctl-upe e1000-rctl-mpe
   in let flags = bit-or e1000-rctl-secrc promisc
   in let rctl = bit-or e1000-rctl-en (bit-or e1000-rctl-bam flags)
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rctl rctl

  e1000-build-rx-descs : E1000Device, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-build-rx-descs (d) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-rx-count then 0
   else let desc = (d.e-rx-ring) + i * e1000-desc-size
   in let buf = (d.e-rx-bufs) + i * e1000-buf-size
   in let lo = poke-32 desc 0 (bit-and buf #FFFFFFFF)
   in let hi = poke-32 (desc + 4) 0 (bit-shru buf 32)
   in let mid = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 0
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in e1000-build-rx-descs d (i + 1)

Section: Transmit Ring

  e1000-setup-tx : E1000Device -> Integer
  e1000-setup-tx (d) =
   let mmio = d.e-mmio
   in let cleared = e1000-zero-words (d.e-tx-ring) (e1000-tx-count * 4) 0
   in let bl = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdbal (bit-and (d.e-tx-ring) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let bh = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdbah (bit-shru (d.e-tx-ring) 32)
   in let ln = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdlen (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size)
   in let h = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdh 0
   in let t = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdt 0
   in let cur = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0 0
   in let g = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tipg e1000-tipg-default
   in let coll = bit-or e1000-tctl-ct e1000-tctl-cold
   in let tctl = bit-or e1000-tctl-en (bit-or e1000-tctl-psp coll)
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tctl tctl

Section: Multicast Table

 The multicast table array is 128 entries of 32 bits and its reset state
 is undefined, so it is cleared before receive is enabled.

  e1000-clear-mta : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-clear-mta (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= 128 then 0
   else let w = e1000-write mmio (e1000-reg-mta + i * 4) 0
   in e1000-clear-mta mmio (i + 1)

Section: Bring-up

  e1000-absent : E1000Device
  e1000-absent =
   E1000Device { e-mmio = 0, e-rx-ring = 0, e-tx-ring = 0, e-rx-bufs = 0,
                 e-tx-bufs = 0, e-ctrl-blk = 0, e-present = False,
                 e-mac = [], e-mac-valid = False }

  e1000-init : PciDevice -> E1000Device
  e1000-init (pci-dev) =
   let enabled = pci-enable-device pci-dev
   in let bar = pci-parse-bar (pci-dev.pci-bus) (pci-dev.pci-dev) (pci-dev.pci-func) 0
   in if e1000-bar-verdict bar /= e1000-bar-ok then e1000-absent
   else e1000-init-at (bar.bar-base)

  e1000-init-at : Integer -> E1000Device
  e1000-init-at (mmio) =
   if e1000-reset mmio == 0 then e1000-absent
   else e1000-init-after-reset (mmio)

  e1000-init-after-reset : Integer -> E1000Device
  e1000-init-after-reset (mmio) =
   let rx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let tx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let rx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let tx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let ctrl-blk = e1000-alloc-aligned e1000-cb-size 8
   in let cb-clear = e1000-zero-words ctrl-blk (e1000-cb-size / 4) 0
   in let mta = e1000-clear-mta mmio 0
   in let mac-ok = e1000-mac-present mmio
   in let mac = if mac-ok then e1000-read-mac mmio else []
   in let d = E1000Device { e-mmio = mmio, e-rx-ring = rx-ring,
                            e-tx-ring = tx-ring, e-rx-bufs = rx-bufs,
                            e-tx-bufs = tx-bufs, e-ctrl-blk = ctrl-blk,
                            e-present = True, e-mac = mac,
                            e-mac-valid = mac-ok }
   in let rx = e1000-setup-rx d
   in let tx = e1000-setup-tx d
   in let link = e1000-link-up mmio
   in d

Section: Receive

  E1000RecvResult = record {
    r-frame : List Integer,
    r-has-frame : Boolean
  }

  e1000-no-frame : E1000RecvResult
  e1000-no-frame = E1000RecvResult { r-frame = [], r-has-frame = False }

 The raw form is the real one and the record form calls it. A caller
 holding the four addresses separately -- the stack does, because it
 keeps no driver record -- would otherwise have to build an E1000Device
 per poll, and a record built per poll is an allocation per poll on a
 path with no collector behind it. That is the same reason the cursors
 live in the control block, so the two decisions are one decision.

  e1000-poll-frame : E1000Device -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-poll-frame (d) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then e1000-no-frame
   else e1000-poll-raw (d.e-mmio) (d.e-rx-ring) (d.e-rx-bufs) (d.e-ctrl-blk)

  e1000-poll-raw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-poll-raw (mmio) (rx-ring) (rx-bufs) (ctrl-blk) =
   if mmio == 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else let idx = bit-and (peek-32 (ctrl-blk + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let desc = rx-ring + idx * e1000-desc-size
   in let status = peek-byte desc 12
   in if bit-and status e1000-rx-status-dd == 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else e1000-take-frame mmio rx-bufs ctrl-blk idx desc status

  e1000-take-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-take-frame (mmio) (rx-bufs) (ctrl-blk) (idx) (desc) (status) =
   let len = bit-or (peek-byte desc 8) (bit-shl (peek-byte desc 9) 8)
   in let buf = rx-bufs + idx * e1000-buf-size
   in let capped = if len > e1000-max-frame then e1000-max-frame else len
   in let whole = bit-and status e1000-rx-status-eop /= 0
   in let frame = if whole then e1000-read-bytes buf 0 capped [] else []
   in let recycled = e1000-recycle-rx mmio ctrl-blk idx desc
   in E1000RecvResult { r-frame = frame, r-has-frame = whole }

 A frame spanning more than one descriptor is dropped rather than
 reassembled, and the descriptor is still returned to the ring. With
 SECRC set and a 2048-byte buffer against a 1522-byte maximum frame,
 the hardware has no reason to split one; dropping is the honest
 handling of a case this driver does not implement, and it cannot
 wedge the ring.

  e1000-recycle-rx : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-recycle-rx (mmio) (ctrl-blk) (idx) (desc) =
   let cleared = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 0
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in let next = int-mod (idx + 1) e1000-rx-count
   in let saved = poke-32 (ctrl-blk + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0 next
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdt idx

  e1000-read-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  e1000-read-bytes (base) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else e1000-read-bytes base (i + 1) len (list-push acc (peek-byte base i))

Section: Transmit

  e1000-send-frame : E1000Device, List Integer -> Integer
  e1000-send-frame (d) (frame) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then 0
   else let len = list-length frame
   in if len <= 0 then 0
   else if len > e1000-max-frame then 0
   else e1000-send-at d frame len

  e1000-send-at : E1000Device, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-send-at (d) (frame) (len) =
   let idx = bit-and (peek-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let desc = (d.e-tx-ring) + idx * e1000-desc-size
   in let buf = (d.e-tx-bufs) + idx * e1000-buf-size
   in let staged = e1000-write-bytes buf frame 0 len
   in let lo = poke-32 desc 0 (bit-and buf #FFFFFFFF)
   in let hi = poke-32 (desc + 4) 0 (bit-shru buf 32)
   in let mid = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 (bit-or (bit-and len #FFFF) (bit-shl e1000-tx-cmd-all 24))
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in let next = int-mod (idx + 1) e1000-tx-count
   in let saved = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0 next
   in let kick = e1000-write (d.e-mmio) e1000-reg-tdt next
   in e1000-await-tx desc 0

 The command byte sits at bit 24 of the third descriptor word, above
 the sixteen-bit length at bit 0 and the checksum-offset byte at bit 16
 which this driver leaves zero.

  e1000-tx-cmd-all : Integer =
   bit-or e1000-tx-cmd-eop (bit-or e1000-tx-cmd-ifcs e1000-tx-cmd-rs)

  e1000-tx-fuel : Integer = 1000000

  e1000-await-tx : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-tx (desc) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-tx-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (peek-byte desc 12) e1000-tx-status-dd /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-tx desc (i + 1)

  e1000-write-bytes : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-write-bytes (base) (bs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0
   else let w = poke-byte base i (list-at bs i)
   in e1000-write-bytes base bs (i + 1) len

